1. Table I is still a claim you're making about competitors from limited evidence. You mark most cells "NR" (not reported), which is honest, but a sharp reviewer will note that "not reported in the cited source" is not the same as "not present in the tool." JFLAP, for instance, almost certainly has some form of exportable diagrams and may have undocumented equivalence-checking behavior. Add one sentence under the table clarifying the scope precisely: "NR reflects documentation coverage in the cited paper, not a claim about undocumented tool capabilities" — you gesture at this in the caption but should say it in prose too, right after the table is introduced, so it isn't missed.

2. The repo link appearing in Section IV-A is good, but it's oddly placed and undersold. Right now it's one clause in the middle of "Architecture and scope" — "Source code and reproducibility scripts are available at [URL]." For a paper leaning this hard on reproducibility (bounded checks, deterministic corpus sampling, audit tests), the repo should be mentioned in the abstract or at minimum flagged more prominently — e.g., a footnote on the first page, which is IEEE convention for artifact/repo links, rather than buried mid-paragraph in Section IV.

3. "Apple M5" and "Node.js v26.1.0" — double check these exist/are correct at time of writing. As of your stated draft date (Sept 27, 2026) I'm not certain an "Apple M5" chip and a "Node.js v26.1.0" release are both real/shipped — this is worth verifying directly rather than me asserting either way, since a factual error in the benchmarking environment description is an easy, embarrassing thing for a reviewer or reader to catch. Confirm the exact chip and Node version you actually benchmarked on.

4. The benchmark methodology needs one more sentence. You say "warmed up 100 times and timed 1,000 times per n" — good — but don't say whether this measures cold construction cost per call (i.e., is there caching between calls that could be inflating or deflating results?), or whether JIT warmup fully stabilizes by 100 iterations. A methods-focused reviewer will ask "did you check for GC pauses / did you report variance, not just median and p95?" You already have p95 in prose — pull it into Table VII as a column so it's not just narrated.

5. Table I's "Independent replay/audit" column — is this really a fair axis to compare on? None of the competitor tools were designed with this as a goal, so "NR" across the board could read as: "we found a metric no one else optimized for and declared ourselves the winner on it." This isn't wrong, but pre-empt the objection with one sentence: explain why replay-audit is a meaningful general property for TOC teaching tools (not just something convenient for your tool to claim), e.g., tie it to the trust/correctness concerns raised by Golesteanu et al.'s ChatGPT-error-rate finding [21] — that's a natural, already-cited hook for "why does independently-verified correctness matter pedagogically."

6. Fig. 6 caption is much better now, but the figure itself (ASCII-math style layout) still looks like a placeholder sketch rather than a real diagram. Given you now have a real repo with D3-rendered diagrams, pull an actual exported diagram from the tool for this figure instead of a schematic — this is the single easiest visual credibility upgrade available to you.

7. Missing: no actual screenshot anywhere still. This was flagged before and remains true in v2. This is the most visible remaining gap for a laboratory/tool paper.

8. The CFG/PDA/TM asymmetry is now well-flagged (good), but consider a cheap partial fix. Even without a full oracle, you could add ONE more independent check for a single model — e.g., for the PDA nⁿbⁿ example, manually verify by an independent script that the reported stack trace is internally consistent (each pop matches a prior push, final stack empty at acceptance). This is much cheaper than a full CFG/PDA/TM oracle suite and would let you say "the CFG/PDA/TM evidence is example-based, except for one internally-audited PDA trace check" — moves the needle from pure limitation to partial mitigation.

9. Numbering/consistency check needed between Table VI and Table VII. In this draft, Table VI covers a*, (a|b)*, etc. (the small examples) and Table VII covers the rₙ family with the new timing column — make sure cross-references in text ("Table VI and Fig. 5 show...") still point to the right tables after this renumbering; with the added Table I you shifted everything down by one, and it's easy to introduce an off-by-one reference error during revision. Worth a careful proofread pass specifically for figure/table number references.

10. Abstract still doesn't mention the repo or reproducibility as a headline feature, despite this being one of your stronger selling points now (deterministic corpus sampling script, audit as permanent regression test, timed benchmarks). Consider one added clause: "...with all code, tests, and the corpus-selection script publicly available for reproduction."